<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Hohengeroldseck</h1><p class="st-meta">1250 CE – 1814 CE (approx.) · capital Seelbach</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tiny Black Forest lordship around the castle of Hohengeroldseck that Napoleon briefly made a sovereign principality of the von der Leyen family.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lords of Geroldseck built the castle of Hohengeroldseck on a ridge between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinzig-rhine">Kinzig</a> and Schutter valleys around 1250. They ruled a small lordship in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ortenau">Ortenau</a> with villages such as Seelbach, held directly from the Empire, while other branches of the family held lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory was tiny and rural, a cluster of farming and forest villages. The Geroldseck family was divided by inheritance disputes, and after their line died out in 1634 the lordship passed to the lords of Cronberg and then, in 1711, to the counts of von der Leyen, who governed it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a> and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saar-river">Saar</a> estates. It remained Catholic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> made it a sovereign Principality of von der Leyen in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, one of its smallest members. It was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1814 and handed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1819. The ruins of Hohengeroldseck castle remain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-hohengeroldseck.jpg" alt="County of Hohengeroldseck" width="320" height="369"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coa_Germany_Family_Hohengeroldseck.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1250 CE – 1814 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Seelbach</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philipp Franz von der Leyen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of von der Leyen, Geroldseck</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1532&amp;focus=county-of-hohengeroldseck" aria-label="County of Hohengeroldseck on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4864 488 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Hohengeroldseck lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5184 744L5229 694M5184 744L5199 691M5184 744L5194 723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5194 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5184 744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5184 744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Seelbach. <a href="/atlas?year=1532&amp;focus=county-of-hohengeroldseck">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
